  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2]?$",
  "worked_solution": "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $t(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=1$ and is not defined for $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)$  is continuous on $[0,2]$ but has a cusp at $x=1.$  Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,2).$  Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$ Hence, it  is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $s(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ but has a corner point at $x=1.$   Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,2).$  Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.",
